  {
    "index": 3,
    "hash": "w+CfxcHrt9K4a1iHCtmQLixGX14=",
    "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-19T11:25:41.426Z",
    "action": {
      "type": "SET_CONTENT",
      "input": {
        "content": "Centric's recorded stack is not a third-party platform at all but 200-plus software products of its own, plus Peppol. The screen's verdict is a single sentence \u2014 it competes as often as it would carry you \u2014 and that is exactly what a portfolio that size implies: on any given deal the firm is at least as likely to be selling its own module as integrating someone else's layer.\n\nThe distinction matters because the partner thesis depends on a firm having a gap it wants filled. A systems integrator carrying a third-party workflow platform has an obvious seam where an append-only record layer can be added without cannibalising anything. A product house has the opposite incentive: whatever the new layer records is a candidate feature for a product it already ships and already bills for.\n\nThe practical consequence is that Centric cannot be qualified from stack data alone. Any future approach would have to be scoped to a specific product line where no internal overlap exists, and that scoping work is heavier than the pipeline can currently justify \u2014 hence priority 9.\n\n---\n\n## Leads sharing this finding (1)\n\n**9 \u00b7 do not pitch**\n- Centric (NL)\n\nVerbatim research text this claim was extracted from:\n\n> Competes as often as it would carry you.",
        "updatedAt": "2026-08-19T11:25:41.426566Z"
      },
      "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-19T11:25:41.426572Z"
    }
  },
